  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[], limit: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "A run of one element always works, so the answer starts at 1 and you are looking for longer runs.",
    "For a fixed left end the balanced property stops holding once the right end grows too far, and the answer for a given right end only shrinks as left moves forward: that monotonicity is what a two-pointer window exploits.",
    "You only ever need the maximum and the minimum of the current window, and each index enters and leaves the window once, so keep two deques of indices holding the candidate extremes at their fronts."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force fixes the left end of the run and walks the right end forward, carrying the running minimum and maximum of the part it has already seen in two variables, so it costs O(n^2) time and O(1) space. The optimal keeps a window [left, right] that is always balanced and stores the indices of the window's extreme candidates in two deques: one whose values decrease from front to back (the window maximum lives at the front) and one whose values increase (the window minimum lives at the front). When the two fronts differ by more than limit, left advances past the offending index, which is popped from the front of whichever deque holds it.",
    "why_optimal": "Each index is pushed once and popped once, so the two deques together do O(n) work over the whole scan and the answer is found in O(n) time with O(n) extra space. O(n) is also the floor here because every element has to be read at least once, and the monotone-shrink argument (left never moves backwards because a longer run can only end further right) is what makes the single pass correct.",
    "pitfalls": "Popping the back of the maximum deque with `<` instead of `<=` keeps equal-valued stale indices alive, and the same mistake with `>` instead of `>=` corrupts the minimum deque. Forgetting to pop an index from the front when left slides past it leaves an index outside the window at the front, which reports a maximum or minimum that is not in the window at all. A single-element input must return 1 rather than 0, and `limit = 0` must still return the longest run of equal values instead of 1."
  },
